<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12313"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 27, 2005 - 4:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The reference to Factor X  may serve two purposes. It allows the BTK Strangler to blame his violent sexual fantasies and desire to murder a particular type of woman on a genetic abnormality. A sadistic psychopath does not accept responsibility for their behavior and actions. Secondly, it allows him to maintain control and domination of the situation. Usually a medical professional diagnoses a patient, but in this case the BTK Strangler made the diagnosis. I suspect Rader does not understand the concept of Factor X. <BR> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
