</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 29, 2005 - 11:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Embersage: Thanks for sharing that very personal side of your life history. It gives us all a new insight into you as a person. I too grew up in a family like that, only way less severe. <BR> <BR>But I don&#39;t think DR&#39;s brothers&#39; reaction is all that odd. After all, who knows how a &#34;normal&#34; person would &#39;typically&#39; react to being told their loveable, straight-laced brother was BTK! <BR> <BR>As for &#34;FACTOR X&#34;: I think it is like Algebra. When you have a quantity &#40;or trait&#41;, that is pretty similar in many situations, you call it &#34;X&#34;. You may not know the exact definition of it, but you know it EXISTS. Like if you people everywhere started getting a purple rash on their index fingers, you might call in &#34;Condition X&#34;. Even though you didn&#39;t know what caused it, it would be easy to just refer to it as &#34;Condition X&#34;. <BR> <BR>Oh, well, that&#39;s my 2 cents anyway. <BR> <BR>Now that I think about it, here&#39;s something really spooky: Charles Manson was on trial for the 7 &#34;Sharon Tate murders&#34; in 1970. In the midst of it, he carved a bloody &#34;X&#34; into his forehead, and released a statement saying he was &#34;X-ing&#34; himself out of this world because he hated it. All his followers, including the 3 girls also on trial, also carved this same &#34;X&#34; into their foreheads. This &#34;X&#34; has stayed on Charlies&#39; face to this day, tho&#39; now he has modified it into a swastika. <BR> <BR>Factor X????? Mwuhahahaha!!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
